  <ym-title|Literature matches for issue #472><label|literature-matches-for-issue-472>

  The proof dependencies and contextual references below have different
  roles. The base manuscript is ef1ea5b1. Historical review fingerprints
  refer to the earlier versions of the changed proofs.

  <block|<tformat|<twith|table-width|1par>|<twith|table-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-hyphen|t>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-valign|bottom>|<table|<row|<cell|<strong|Source
  and locator>>|<cell|<strong|Use and hypothesis
  check>>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|Fernández\UProcacci, arXiv
  preprint|https://arxiv.org/pdf/math-ph/0605041>, Ÿ4.1, Proposition 5,
  (4.3)>|<cell|The alternating sum over connected spanning subgraphs of a
  finite graph equals the signed number of Penrose trees. Apply it to the
  graph of numbered occurrences, so repeated polymer labels are distinct
  vertices and their incompatibility is an ordinary edge. A disconnected
  graph gives zero on both sides. The weighted rooted forest estimate and
  passage to the infinite alphabet stay in the
  manuscript.>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|Hall, arXiv:math-ph/0005032|https://arxiv.org/pdf/math-ph/0005032>,
  Definitions 2.2 and 3.13, Theorems 3.6 and 3.23>|<cell|The local
  homeomorphism theorem and the two ambient log/exp identities give the
  neighborhood statement used in the normal chart. The local logarithm
  identifies a small ambient logarithm with the algebra logarithm, giving the
  reverse implication. Compare the archived v1 locators already checked in
  the G03 ledger. This applies even for disconnected G. It does not give the
  nearest-point property or uniform chart estimates by
  itself.>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|DLMF Ÿ10.32|https://dlmf.nist.gov/10.32#E8>,
  <hlink|Ÿ10.31|https://dlmf.nist.gov/10.31#E1>,
  <hlink|Ÿ10.25|https://dlmf.nist.gov/10.25#E2>,
  <hlink|Ÿ10.27|https://dlmf.nist.gov/10.27#E4>>|<cell|The integral uses the
  positive argument ms and order (j+2)/2. The integer and noninteger series
  have integer exponents after extracting the prefactor because twice the
  order is integral. We differentiate actual analytic series factors and
  control their tails rather than differentiating an unspecified asymptotic
  expansion. The massless case is elementary. The final exclusion argument is
  specific to this manuscript.>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|Munthe-Kaas\UVerdier\UVilmart
  v2|https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.01588v2>, Theorem 1>|<cell|The pairing
  formula is for centered real Gaussian vectors. The indicated
  polynomial-moment limit covers singular covariance and multilinearity
  covers complex coordinates. This citation is for the pairing rule, not the
  weighted moment bounds.>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|Jaffard, journal
  scan|https://www.numdam.org/item/AIHPC_1990__7_5_461_0.pdf>, Proposition 2,
  p. 464>|<cell|Exponential decay of the inverse under summability of
  exponential distance weights and invertibility on the coordinate Hilbert
  space. The displayed metric and multiplicity check explain the qualitative
  match. The proof with explicit constants uniform over torus periods and
  principal windows stays.>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|Osterwalder\USeiler, publisher
  record and abstract|https://doi.org/10.1016/0003-4916(78)90039-8>>|<cell|Historical
  physical-positivity attribution. No theorem application to the arbitrary
  matrix representation or to either of the two periodic reflection cuts is
  claimed from the abstract. Both cuts are proved in the
  manuscript.>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|Osterwalder\USchrader
  I|https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01645738>, Ÿ3>|<cell|The reflected form follows
  their Euclidean construction. The checked background record gives the
  comparison of test spaces and hypotheses. The contractivity proof and later
  reconstruction remain on the manuscript's
  tables.>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|Baªaban, RG
  I|https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01215223>, ŸŸ0\U2>|<cell|Small-field localized
  effective actions and fluctuation integration are the structural precedent.
  The original pages were checked in the gauge-averaging and G04 records. The
  marked-observable budgets and global schedule have their own
  proofs.>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|Hall\UWightman, original Royal Danish Academy
  scan|https://gymarkiv.sdu.dk/MFM/kdvs/mfm%2030-39/mfm-31-5.pdf>, synopsis
  and parts I\UIII>|<cell|The paper starts from Lorentz-invariant analytic
  functions and discusses scalar vacuum correlations. The present argument
  starts with separated Euclidean tables and constructs a full tube with
  field labels and tested boundary values. The reference gives context, not
  that construction.>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|Hollands\UKopper
  v3|https://arxiv.org/pdf/1105.3375v3>, Ÿ1>|<cell|Spectator-tested OPE
  remainders in massive Euclidean phi-four at finite loop order. The
  Wilson-limit result uses an independent nonperturbative proof and fixed
  truncation degree.>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|Caracciolo\UCurci\UMenotti\UPelissetto,
  author institution record|https://iris.uniroma1.it/handle/11573/74973> and
  <hlink|journal DOI|https://doi.org/10.1016/0003-4916(90)90203-Z>>|<cell|Metadata
  and subject verified. The journal full text was unavailable in this pass.
  The reference is for precedent on the lattice energy\Umomentum tensor, with
  no claim that it proves the continuum normalization, Ward limits or trace
  asymptotic here.>>>>>

  Hall and Jaffard now have bibliography citations. The two Lebl citations in
  the running-coupling pilot were verified by the integrated bibliography
  build, so the entry remains. This pass does not replace the other candidate
  proofs in the issue. In particular a scalar tube theorem alone would not
  provide the tested continuation and label covariance needed in the
  reconstruction chapters.

  The 1,241-page monograph builds with no unresolved citations or references,
  duplicate labels, or overfull boxes. The blueprint and whitespace checks
  pass.

  <section|Continuation: disposition of every proposal
  row><label|continuation-disposition-of-every-proposal-row>

  \PRetain\Q means the local proof carries a hypothesis, bound or
  identification that the external result does not supply directly, or that
  the proposed edition or statement has not been checked. The table records
  the limits of this search, including leads that could not be checked at
  theorem level.

  <block|<tformat|<twith|table-width|1par>|<twith|table-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-hyphen|t>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-valign|bottom>|<table|<row|<cell|<strong|Proposal
  in #472>>|<cell|<strong|Disposition>>>|<row|<cell|Lattice reflection and
  transfer matrix>|<cell|Osterwalder\USeiler is cited from the publisher
  record and abstract. Lüscher's 1977 publication is confirmed
  (<hlink|DOI|https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01614090>), but we did not verify a
  statement covering bounded Borel observables, both cuts on an even torus,
  and arbitrary compact matrix groups in the given representation. Seiler LNP
  159 was not checked to that scope. Retain the finite-period
  proof.>>|<row|<cell|Euclidean Hilbert space, contraction, semigroup and
  bumps>|<cell|Cite OS I Ÿ3 with the explicit slab-bound and separated-test
  adaptation. The corrected OS II reconstruction hypotheses are compared in
  the existing background note. Klein\ULandau's 1981 paper on the circle
  treats Gaussian covariances at positive temperature, while their KMS work
  concerns a different input. Neither supplies the selected separated-table
  construction. No Glimm\UJaffe theorem substituted without matching the test
  domain.>>|<row|<cell|Ground projection>|<cell|Replaced the large-time limit
  proof by the bounded Borel calculus in Reed\USimon I, Theorem VII.2. The
  extra steps identify the spectral projection of the time-one operator at 1
  with the common fixed space and bound the fractional-time remainder by
  contractivity.>>|<row|<cell|Complex heat>|<cell|Reed\USimon I, Theorem
  VII.2 gives the bounded Borel calculus. Using the actual time-one operator
  and its injectivity identifies the original real semigroup and fixes the
  sign of the unitary multiplier. The compact half-plane bound gives norm
  holomorphy.>>|<row|<cell|Ground commutativity and pure
  phases>|<cell|Gelfand and Riesz already have precise references at their
  uses. Abstract commutative representation does not prove that the concrete
  compressed slab operators commute, that the vacuum measure has full
  support, or that the phase fibers have the required vacuum line. Retain.
  The suggested Fröhlich\USimon citation is ambiguous. The checked
  Fröhlich\USimon\USpencer paper on infrared bounds is CMP 50 (1976), with
  spin-system hypotheses, rather than a theorem on this ground algebra. No
  Bratteli\URobinson or Glimm\UJaffe replacement is
  claimed.>>|<row|<cell|Tempered tube boundary and Fourier support>|<cell|The
  general tube/Paley\UWiener references are relevant, but no checked
  statement supplies the common tested weak-height extension and its
  uniformity over phases. Retain the joint Taylor remainder and cutoff
  argument. Hörmander, Vladimirov, Reed\USimon II and the Streater\UWightman
  edition locators remain leads for this exact
  comparison.>>|<row|<cell|Lorentz covariance>|<cell|Hall\UWightman is cited
  from the original text. Its scalar invariant tube analysis starts with
  Lorentz invariance. Here the calibrated finite-grade label action and
  covariance are derived from Euclidean data first. Zinoviev's theorem 4.1
  uses E5 (see the checked OS background record). Keep the direct
  proof.>>|<row|<cell|Spacelike exchange and Jost configurations>|<cell|The
  Hall\UWightman original discusses spacelike real sets in the scalar
  invariant theory. A Jost-point theorem or Rudin's edge-of-the-wedge theorem
  would still require the two ordered continuations, equality on the relevant
  open real patch and control of their boundary tests with spectators. Retain
  the geometry and tested exchange. Rudin CBMS 6 and Jost's book are verified
  as publication leads, not substituted at theorem level.>>|<row|<cell|Word
  fields, motion, locality>|<cell|Cite Wightman's original
  <hlink|Ÿ6|https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRev.101.860>, which reconstructs the
  cyclic neutral scalar theory from its vacuum functions. Keep the graded
  common domain, mixed tests and relation to the actual boundary values. No
  direct invocation of a Streater\UWightman or Bogolubov\ULogunov\UTodorov
  theorem with an unchecked label match.>>|<row|<cell|Tensor
  tests>|<cell|Replaced the density proof with Trèves, Theorem 51.6, Eq.
  (51.3), checked in the original text. Iterate the two-factor isomorphism,
  using continuity of multiplication on separate slots. The elementary
  seminorm estimate supplies that continuity and the empty tuple is
  scalar.>>|<row|<cell|Affine motion on Schwartz space>|<cell|The elementary
  chain rule and polynomial weight comparison give continuity together with
  the parameter dependence used for the group action. Retain the short check.
  No unverified Reed\USimon or Hörmander locator needed.>>|<row|<cell|Upper
  half-plane support, SNAG, cluster and vacuum>|<cell|The manuscript cites
  BHV for the joint spectral measure. Retain the sign check in the Paley
  argument and the distinction between the selected and every-character
  vacuum conclusions. Araki\UHepp\URuelle treats spacelike asymptotics of
  Wightman functions. It does not construct the field representations or give
  the uniform character heat estimate used to extend the vacuum
  conclusion.>>|<row|<cell|Ordered extraction and smooth grid
  limits>|<cell|Rudin, Theorem 7.23, gives the bounded countable diagonal
  step. Theorem 7.25 applies to continuous equicontinuous sequences on a
  compact set. The rounded grid functions first satisfy a weaker asymptotic
  modulus, so keep the finite-net upgrade, derivative comparison and ordered
  period/mesh argument.>>|<row|<cell|Mass-shell profiles>|<cell|Replaced the
  radial asymptotic analysis by the checked Bessel formulas and supplied
  derivative-tail control. The finite-profile obstruction stays. Watson's
  lemma concerns a different endpoint expansion in its usual large-parameter
  form.>>|<row|<cell|Haar density in exponential coordinates>|<cell|Replaced
  the local translation averaging argument using Duistermaat\UKolk, Theorem
  1.5.3, Eq. (1.5.1), checked in the original text. Its left-trivialized
  exponential differential is exactly the derivative of the manuscript's
  chart translation. The left-invariant density and Haar uniqueness supply
  the missing probability normalization, including disconnected groups. No
  Helgason locator required.>>|<row|<cell|Maximal-tree gauge
  fixing>|<cell|Creutz 1977 is the relevant precedent. The finite
  rooted-forest proof additionally matches the two coordinate Hessians and
  cancels their determinants against the chart Jacobian. Keep this
  comparison. The checked Baªaban background record concerns covariant block
  averaging and normalized gauge fixing, rather than this determinant
  identity.>>|<row|<cell|Radial gauge>|<cell|Retain the short radial
  transport identity and the weighted-jet reconstruction. The usual radial
  gauge formula is a starting point only. Uhlenbeck's local Coulomb gauge
  theorem does not supply a radial Taylor identity. No original Fock,
  Schwinger or Shifman locator is claimed here.>>|<row|<cell|Exterior
  exactness>|<cell|Barnich\UBrandt\UHenneaux, Theorem 4.2, concerns the
  horizontal differential on local jet forms. The manuscript uses lattice
  Fourier multipliers with prescribed strip bounds and cancellation at zero
  momentum. A Koszul regular-sequence argument can explain algebraic
  exactness locally but would still require the quantitative divisions and
  global periodicity. Retain.>>|<row|<cell|Positive Laurent
  factors>|<cell|Cite Gröchenig\ULeinert, Theorem 6 and the
  exponential-weight caveat after Corollary 7, from the journal article. The
  GRS condition fails at a fixed exponential weight. The narrower-strip
  estimate with a width picked uniformly from the spectral and derivative
  bounds is the adaptation.>>|<row|<cell|Local inverses>|<cell|Cite
  Demko\UMoss\USmith, Theorem 2.4, and Jaffard, Proposition 2. Bandwidth in a
  linear order is different from decay in anchor distance. Jaffard supplies
  the qualitative decay mechanism but the uniform all-period and window
  estimates are needed explicitly. The exponential conjugation is of
  Combes\UThomas type, not an application of their multiparticle Schrödinger
  theorem to these matrices.>>|<row|<cell|Strip Fourier
  estimates>|<cell|Retain contour shifting and its explicit dimension/width
  constants. Katznelson is a harmonic-analysis reference, but no verified
  statement was identified with the required multi-variable width and
  weighted Laurent sum.>>|<row|<cell|Weighted convolution and Schur
  estimates>|<cell|The submultiplicativity calculation applies to row kernels
  on periodized lattices and carries the exact weights. Retain the short
  calculation. An unweighted Young inequality does not give that assertion
  without the same weight splitting.>>|<row|<cell|Analytic inverse and
  implicit function>|<cell|The contraction proof already supplies the
  analytic inverse in the exact real structure and the block
  implicit-function reduction. Krantz\UParks Ÿ2.5 was located, but its
  theorem text and edition-specific numbering were not verified here. Keep
  the short proof.>>|<row|<cell|Gaussian calculus>|<cell|Cite the checked
  Isserlis theorem for joint moments. The independent-noise limit and
  multilinearity give the singular and complex cases. Keep covariance
  differentiation and reassembly where the local cutoff weights, moving chart
  and support bookkeeping matter. Bauerschmidt\UBrydges\USlade is a relevant
  Gaussian-integration comparison, not a source for those geometric
  weights.>>|<row|<cell|Polymer expansions>|<cell|Cite Kotecký\UPreiss for
  the abstract expansion and Fernández\UProcacci for the exact Penrose
  identity. Keep the occurrence-by-occurrence geometric majorant, which the
  later derivatives use. Friedli\UVelenik chapter 5 and Brydges's Les Houches
  lectures are relevant background. The C08 note compares the abstract
  criterion to the actual inputs.>>|<row|<cell|Stationary determinant and
  contact>|<cell|The stationary Gaussian determinant is the usual Laplace
  calculation, and the Haar factor accompanies the change of coordinates.
  Keep the contact-term cancellation and core comparison, which are not
  supplied by a generic Laplace or Faddeev\UPopov reference. No unverified
  Bleistein\UHandelsman locator inserted.>>|<row|<cell|RG
  attribution>|<cell|Cite Baªaban directly at the RG construction. The
  existing source note gives checked contour, averaging and gauge-fixing
  comparisons. Dimock I\UIII and Gaw¦dzki\UKupiainen concern different models
  or RG choices, and Bauerschmidt\UBrydges\USlade develops a general RG
  method in a different setting. None supplies the marked Wilson transitions,
  the selected scale schedule or their bounds.>>|<row|<cell|One-loop
  running>|<cell|Cite Lüscher\UWeisz, hep-lat/9505011 Ÿ1, for perturbative
  Wilson SU(N) matching. Dashen\UGross and Hasenfratz\UHasenfratz also study
  lattice/continuum coupling comparison. This is different from a
  finite-volume error bound for the selected Wilson family. Retain the
  monograph's Casimir normalization and the derivation of the uniform
  shooting slope. No equality of bare-coupling conventions is inferred from
  the reference.>>|<row|<cell|Maxwell tests>|<cell|The Gaussian pairing
  citation supports ordinary Gaussian moments. Retain the free Maxwell
  curvature and Ward computations because they identify the precise grade and
  all-spectator tests in the stated normalization. An abstract free-field
  chapter is insufficient for these identifications.>>|<row|<cell|Lattice
  stress normalization>|<cell|Caracciolo\UCurci\UMenotti\UPelissetto is cited
  for precedent. The local charge comparison and selected continuum
  normalization remain proved.>>|<row|<cell|OPE>|<cell|Hollands\UKopper is
  cited with its perturbative scope stated. Wilson\UZimmermann concerns the
  short-distance expansion and composite operators. Neither supplies the
  phase-uniform, fixed-truncation estimates on this Wilson
  hierarchy.>>|<row|<cell|Trace anomaly and trace asymptotic>|<cell|Cite
  Collins\UDuncan\UJoglekar from the publisher's abstract for non-Abelian
  anomaly context. Adler\UCollins\UDuncan is the spin-one-half QED result and
  is not needed for the non-Abelian attribution. Neither proves the selected
  short-distance two-point limit.>>|<row|<cell|Bibliography and standard
  names>|<cell|The build confirms Hall, Jaffard and Lebl are all cited.
  Existing locators for Williams, Sawyer, Melrose, Tropp and BHV remain
  attached to the statements they support. A published-text substitution
  needs a checked locator and convention match. Bochner integration,
  finite-dimensional Schur calculations and the Neumann series are routine
  methods in the retained proofs, not appeals to unverified specialized
  results.>>>>>

  Primary texts inspected in the continuation include the <hlink|Trèves
  scan|https://rexresearch1.com/TopologyLibrary/TopologicalVectorSpacesTreves.pdf>,
  <hlink|Reed\USimon I|https://www.astrosen.unam.mx/~aceves/Metodos/ebooks/reed_simon1.pdf>,
  <hlink|Duistermaat\UKolk|https://webusers.imj-prg.fr/~julien.marche/M1/duistermaatkolk.pdf>,
  <hlink|Rudin|https://www.lehman.edu/faculty/rbettiol/lehman_teaching/2020mat320/baby_Rudin.pdf>,
  <hlink|Gröchenig\ULeinert|https://archiv.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/volltextserver/29187/1/Leinert_2006_Schriften25.pdf>,
  <hlink|Demko\UMoss\USmith|https://www.ams.org/mcom/1984-43-168/S0025-5718-1984-0758197-9/S0025-5718-1984-0758197-9.pdf>,
  the <hlink|Kotecký\UPreiss article scan|https://www.math.ucla.edu/~biskup/290k.1.24w/PDFs/Kotecky-Preiss.pdf>,
  and <hlink|Barnich\UBrandt\UHenneaux v3|https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0002245>.
  See the table for the exact locators and proof uses.

  Continuation validation: the rebuilt PDF has 1,240 pages and no unresolved
  references or citations, duplicate labels, or overfull boxes. The revised
  complex heat, tensor-test and Haar-density pages were rendered and
  inspected. The blueprint checker and whitespace check pass.
